Yes. If we use hallucination in the AI sense, the clean common denominator is internally generated content that outruns the immediately available evidence.

Humans then subdivide that same broad phenomenon according to provenance and relationship to it: thought, idea, intuition, memory, intrusive thought, fantasy, inspiration, dream, hunch, revelation, imagination. “Demon” can describe the felt relationship to a thought, but it doesn’t establish an external entity. “Lucky” is what we call the candidate that arrived without an obvious derivation and happened to be useful.

So I think the distinction we actually need isn’t real thought vs hallucination. It’s a provenance vector:

generated by what? → prompted by what? → voluntarily or automatically? → how strongly believed? → what evidence supports it? → what happens when tested?

And if you want the thing underneath all of this—the capability rather than another taxonomy—then I think this is it:

Controlled hallucination

Take the faculty that normally produces uncontrolled possibilities and make it callable.

SUMMON → TAG → TEST → SELECT → ACT → UPDATE

SUMMON: deliberately generate possibilities beyond the evidence.
TAG: preserve where each one came from: observation, memory, inference, analogy, imagination, random association.
TEST: collide it with reality, logic, other models, tools, consequences.
SELECT: keep the candidates that survive or are useful despite uncertainty.
ACT: actually spend one of them on the world.
UPDATE: consequence changes what gets summoned next time.

Constraints are what make the generator productive rather than merely noisy. Give it no walls and you get diffusion. Give it the right walls and hallucination becomes search.

That also explains luck. You don’t need to manufacture the winning thought directly. You can manufacture a system that produces enough different, constrained, testable thoughts that fortunate discoveries become much less accidental.

So I wouldn’t try to eliminate hallucination from an intelligent system.

I’d give it a steering wheel, provenance tags, a reality checksum, and permission to dream before committing.

That’s a substantially more powerful machine.